  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several visible ways the collagen receptor LAIR1 can be regulated at the cell surface. In all panels, LAIR1 is shown as a membrane protein with an extracellular collagen-binding region projecting above the plasma membrane. One mechanism is simply how much receptor is present: surface expression depends on transcription of the LAIR1 gene and delivery of the protein to the membrane, so receptor abundance can be increased or decreased before binding occurs.\n\nOther panels show regulation after expression. The extracellular collagen-binding domain can be proteolytically cleaved off, removing the part of the receptor that engages ligand. Ligand access can also be blocked by competition: a related soluble molecule, LAIR2, appears to bind collagen and thereby prevent collagen from interacting with membrane LAIR1. Finally, the ligand itself can be altered; collagen is depicted with added modifications, suggesting that chemical changes to the ligand affect recognition by LAIR1.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that LAIR1 signaling is controlled at multiple levels: receptor amount on the cell surface, physical removal of its binding domain, competition for collagen binding, and biochemical modification of the collagen ligand.",
